  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "aws-modern-governed-data-platform-v1",
  "title": "Governed data platform spanning lake, streams, search, and warehouse",
  "subtitle": "A layered data estate combines scalable object storage, catalog and access governance, stream processing, search, and analytic serving. Synthetic Azure reference; no deployment is implied.",
  "description": "### Problem\nAn enterprise wants to make governed data reusable across operational producers, analytics teams, and business consumers without forcing every workload into a single storage or query engine.\n\n### Flow and Azure mapping\nBatch and streaming producers land data in Azure Data Lake Storage or OneLake. Azure Data Factory, Event Hubs, and stream processing services feed and transform the data. A catalog and governance layer classifies data and coordinates access; curated datasets are served through Fabric Warehouse, Azure Data Explorer, Azure AI Search, or purpose-built stores. BI and ML consumers use approved interfaces. Select only components relevant to a particular implementation.\n\n### Trade-offs and assumptions\nThe AWS guide is a broad architecture overview, not a single deployable topology. The Azure graph is intentionally a composable platform pattern, and services such as Lake Formation, Glue, Redshift, OpenSearch, and Kinesis do not map one-to-one to Azure products. Data ownership, encryption, consent/privacy, retention, lineage, and quality are organizational practices as well as service settings.\n\nSource: [AWS Prescriptive Guidance](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/strategy-aws-data/aws-architecture.html).\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- Origin provider: AWS\n- Target: Azure conceptual adaptation\n- Primary source: [AWS modern data architecture](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/strategy-aws-data/aws-architecture.html)\n- Reviewed: 2026-11-06\n\n## Catalog status\n\nThis is an original, editable Azure conceptual adaptation. It is synthetic reference material, not a deployed system, certified vendor architecture, or automatic deployment plan.\n\n## Mapping caveats\n\n- This is a portfolio pattern, not a single product stack; avoid suggesting all stores and services are required.",
